{"qid":"Q26472","names":["Kingdom of Dali"],"title":"Kingdom of Dali","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Dali was a Bai dynasty state centered on the Erhai lake basin in what is now Yunnan, China, founded in 937 by Duan Siping after the collapse of the earlier Nanzhao kingdom. Under its twenty-two Duan rulers it controlled overland trade routes between China, Southeast Asia, and Tibet and maintained a distinctly Buddhist political culture, with Duan kings often abdicating to become monks. Mongol forces under Kublai Khan invaded in 1253, capturing the capital Dali in early 1254; the last king, Duan Xingzhi, submitted and was kept on as a local administrator, ending Dali's independence. Our dataset attests the kingdom to 1259, consistent with the final pacification of Yunnan.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core territory around the Erhai basin is well attested through Chinese dynastic records and local stone inscriptions; the northern and eastern extents into the Yunnan plateau are reconstructed from tribute and military records and carry moderate uncertainty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Dali Kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dali_Kingdom"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Dali Kingdom","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Duan Siping (Emperor Shengshen / Taizu)","nativeName":"段思平","roman":"Duàn Sīpíng","start":937,"end":944,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Duan Siying (Emperor Wensheng)","nativeName":"段思英","roman":"Duàn Sīyīng","start":944,"end":945,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"